KG42.register("rules-634ea6420b2589f9e866c1b8",{"id":"38-bidirectional_ev_trading::v141-c-R009","paper":"38-bidirectional_ev_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R009","mechanism_id":"M02","name":"Energy-trade execution","process_stage":"activation","market_meaning":"Customers respond to the higher overall electricity rates caused by increasing alpha by reducing the amount of energy they trade. Scope: Reported non-collaborative Fig. 6 numerical comparison of the three EV-type cases; customer-aggregator energy trading. Condition: The penalizing factor alpha increases within the reported parameter sweep.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function execution.energy_trade under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.price_signal","execution.energy_trade"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R009-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"execution.energy_trade","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R009-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E19"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Reported non-collaborative Fig. 6 numerical comparison of the three EV-type cases; customer-aggregator energy trading.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E19"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-X001","symbol":"Q_trade,c","meaning":"Average total amount of energy trading in the non-collaborative system for reported EV-type case c, treated as a function of the penalizing factor alpha in the Fig. 6 numerical comparison.","kind":"operational","domain":"Reported average total energy-trading values (kWh) for the NCS Fig. 6 cases; no closed-form functional domain is supplied.","unit":"kWh","indices":["EV-type case c"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E19","v141-b-g03-E18"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-T001","symbol":"α","meaning":"Penalizing factor of the tiered proportional billing scheme in the non-collaborative system; increasing α raises overall electricity rates in the reported Fig. 6 comparison.","value":{"kind":"text","number_value":null,"text_value":"reported sweep from 1 to 3 in Fig. 6; no single fixed value"},"unit":"dimensionless","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E19"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Customers respond to the higher overall electricity rates caused by increasing alpha by reducing the amount of energy they trade. Scope: Reported non-collaborative Fig. 6 numerical comparison of the three EV-type cases; customer-aggregator energy trading. Condition: The penalizing factor alpha increases within the reported parameter sweep.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"In the reported non-collaborative Fig. 6 comparison, for each reported EV-type case the average total amount of energy trading is non-increasing as the penalizing factor α increases over the reported sweep from 1 to 3. This is a weak monotonic formalization of the source statement that customers 'monotonically reduce' energy trading because higher α raises overall electricity rates; the source does not specify a functional form or strictness.","expression":"∀ c ∈ {Type I, Type II, Type III}, ∀ α₁, α₂ ∈ [1,3]: α₁ \u003c α₂ ⇒ Q_trade,c(α₂) ≤ Q_trade,c(α₁).","formalization_origin":"equivalent_translation","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-X001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-T001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E19"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p12-c2r4__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":12,"printed_page":null,"section":"VI. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"Fig. 6(a) shows the social welfare of three cases with different types of EVs varying α from 1 to 3.","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-12.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Fig. 6(a) shows the social welfare of three cases with different types of EVs varying \\alpha from 1 to 3. For all cases, as \\alpha increases, the social welfare increases up to a certain point and then decreases. This implies that we can improve the social welfare of the non-collaborative system by appropriately choosing \\alpha. However, as \\alpha increases, the customers monotonically reduce the amount of their energy consumption as well as the amount of energy trading, as shown in Figs. 6(b) and 6(c), since the increase in \\alpha raises the overall electricity rates of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p11-c2r1__v141-a-M02-settlement","page":11,"printed_page":null,"section":"VI. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"SCHEDULING WITH ELECTRIC VEHICLES IN THE SMART GRID 1229 TABLE III PERFORMANCE COMPARISON OF THE COL","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"TABLE III","image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"TABLE III PERFORMANCE COMPARISON OF THE COLLABORATIVE SYSTEM (CS) AND THE NON-COLLABORATIVE SYSTEM (NCS). Type of EVs Type I Type II Type III CS / NCS CS / NCS CS / NCS. Social welfare 2.25 / -1.55 16.13 / 14.10 24.84 / 20.12. Total utility 53.15 / 63.12 61.37 / 75.30 61.49 / 75.11. Total demand (kWh) 142.36 / 159.50 137.36 / 159.67 123.87 / 151.23. Total energy trading (kWh) 0 / 0 0 / 0 27.86 / 11.77. Total cost ($) 50.90 / 64.68 45.24 / 61.21 36.64 / 55.00. Average wholesale price ($/kWh) 0.36 / 0.40 0.33 / 0.38 0.29 / 0.36. Sum of bills ($) 98.96 / 64.68 87.73 / 61.21 70.82 / 55.00."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M02-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E19","quote":"However, as \\alpha increases, the customers monotonically reduce the amount of their energy consumption as well as the amount of energy trading, as shown in Figs. 6(b) and 6(c), since the increase in \\alpha raises the overall electricity rates of the power system."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"37173247798349b7f04f6772eeec7063c390483dab35fc1f1a0e6e4913804a21","applicability":"formalized","reason":"E19 reports that, in the non-collaborative Fig. 6 comparison, customers monotonically reduce energy trading as alpha increases over the reported 1-to-3 sweep because higher alpha raises overall electricity rates. This is formalized only as a weak non-increasing reported relation; the source does not supply a closed-form trading function, strict monotonicity, or an optimization condition. The B evidence remains unreviewed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-X001","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001"],"rationale":"The variable is the amount of energy trading, which is the target quantity on the frozen energy-trade execution node; the edge represents its dependence on the elevated price signal."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-T001","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001"],"rationale":"Alpha is the billing penalizing factor whose increase raises overall electricity rates; it is attached to the price-signal side of the frozen relation, without adding a new node."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001"],"rationale":"The original procedural statement is retained unchanged and anchored to the same frozen edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R009-E001"],"rationale":"The formal monotonic inequality relates the price-signal driver to the energy-trade quantity along the existing edge; it is a reported numerical response, not a new optimization constraint."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
